<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 1940s: the wartime interruption, the return of the Giro and Tour, the Bartali–Coppi rivalry and Campagnolo’s mechanical intermediate steps."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1940-1949/efterkrigsfelt-illustration.jpg"/><title>When war closed the roads · 1940–1949 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1940-1949/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1940-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1940-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 1940–1949</p><h1>When war closed<br/><em>the roads</em></h1></div><p>The major stage races disappeared. When they returned, cycle racing reflected both continuity and reconstruction.</p></header>
<figure class="decade1970-hero-image"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1940-1949/efterkrigsfelt-illustration.jpg`} alt="Illustration of a peloton on a worn Italian road shortly after the Second World War"/><figcaption>A general post-war scene with bicycles and clothing typical of the period. AI-generated illustration — not an image of a specific race or named riders.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1940-lead"><p class="section-label">A break in the middle of the calendar</p><p>A decade is normally a continuation. The 1940s first became an interruption. Races, factories, newspapers and the lives of riders were drawn into the war — and the sport later had to rebuild its rhythm.</p></section>
<section class="era-facts decade1970-facts" aria-label="Highlights from cycle racing in the 1940s">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">1940–1945</p><h2>The grand tours disappear</h2></div><div><p>Fausto Coppi won the Giro d’Italia in 1940 at just 20 years of age. The race was then suspended until 1946. The Tour de France had last been held in 1939 and did not return until 1947.</p><p>Cycle racing did not cease entirely. Local and national races were organised when conditions allowed, but the international calendar had broken down. Borders, transport, fuel, materials and security imposed entirely different limits from those before the war.</p><p>For many riders, the lost seasons were not merely a gap in a results list. They fell in the middle of the few years when a professional would normally be at his physical best.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">1946</p><h2>The Giro returns</h2></div><div><p>When the Giro d’Italia was held again in 1946, its route crossed a country with visible war damage and deep political tensions. <em>La Gazzetta dello Sport</em> had resumed publication in 1945 and announced the race’s return in January 1946.</p><p>Gino Bartali won ahead of Fausto Coppi. Their rivalry had roots before the war, but now acquired a meaning that extended beyond the two riders. Bartali was often presented as the traditional and devout hero; Coppi as the lean, modern and methodical challenger. Reality was more complicated, but the contrast made good newspaper copy.</p><p>Coppi won the Giro the following year. In 1949, he won both the Giro d’Italia and the Tour de France and became the first rider to take the two major stage races in the same year.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1940-return"><div><span>1947</span><h2>The Tour finds a new winner</h2><p>Jean Robic reached the final stage without ever having worn the yellow jersey. An attack on the road to Paris overturned the standings, and he won the first post-war Tour.</p></div><div><span>1948</span><h2>Bartali, ten years later</h2><p>Gino Bartali won the Tour de France for the second time — ten years after his victory in 1938. His attacks in the Alps decided the race after rain, cold and snow in the mountains.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">Mechanics between two ages</p><h2>The wheel still had to be loosened</h2><p>In 1946, Campagnolo applied the name Cambio Corsa to its two-lever system. The rider could change between sprockets without dismounting, but had to reach backwards, release the rear axle, move the chain and tighten the wheel again while the bicycle was rolling.</p><p>The design is often called the “suicide shifter” today. That was not its usual contemporary name. For professionals of the time, it was a demanding but usable step between the reversible rear wheel and the spring-loaded derailleur.</p><p>In 1949 came the single-lever system later known as Paris–Roubaix. It simplified operation, but the rear wheel still had to move to keep the chain tensioned.</p></div><aside aria-label="Typical details on a 1940s racing bicycle"><span>Steel frame</span><span>Tubular tyres</span><span>Few sprockets</span><span>Lever-operated gear</span><span>Wool jersey</span></aside></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Last place becomes visible</p><h2>The black jersey</h2></div><div><p>When the Giro returned in 1946, the black jersey was associated with the rider at the bottom of the general classification. Luigi Malabrocca finished last in both 1946 and 1947 and became the first major figure in the jersey’s peculiar afterlife.</p><p>Prizes and publicity made last place attractive enough for some riders to try to lose time without exceeding the time limit. There are many stories of hiding places, punctures and deliberately slow riding, but the individual incidents deserve to be investigated as stories in their own right.</p><p>In 1948, Aldo Bini finished last after completing the race with a broken hand. His position was therefore quite different from a comic contest to progress as slowly as possible.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Food, medicine and experience</p><h2>Before sports science became a system</h2></div><div><p>Riders’ diets relied more on tradition, team habits and what could be obtained than on precise plans for carbohydrates and fluids. Bread, rice, pasta, eggs and meat appeared in large meals, while coffee, wine and other drinks could also accompany riders through long days.</p><p>It is tempting to turn the period into a parade of steaks and red wine, but wartime and post-war rationing made reality far less uniform. Access to food was itself part of the conditions.</p><p>Stimulants had long been used in endurance sport. Amphetamine was used by the military during the war and spread through civilian life and sport after 1945. There was not yet an effective anti-doping system, but this does not mean that every precise cocktail recipe or later quotation from a rider can serve as reliable evidence for the 1940s.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-feature decade1940-related"><div><p class="section-label">See the period in the metal</p><h2>From overview to object</h2><p>The period stands as its own account. The related pages look more closely at specific bicycles, components and people without copying them into the period chapter.</p></div><div class="period-feature-links"><a href={`${base}/en/components/campagnolo-cambio-corsa/`}><span>Component · 1946</span><strong>Campagnolo Cambio Corsa</strong><b>See the mechanics →</b></a><a href={`${base}/en/stories/aldo-bini-maglia-nera-1948/`}><span>Story · 1948 Giro</span><strong>Aldo Bini and the black jersey</strong><b>Read the story →</b></a></div></section>
